    "updated_at": "2026-11-19T06:26:40Z",
    "body": "fix on issue/2683-probe-rotation-guard · PR #2711 · pinned by setting_the_active_offset_stops_when_it_is_rotated, setting_another_offset_stops_when_either_is_rotated, the_guard_follows_the_sections_work_offset, the_guard_is_written_once_under_its_own_o_number, a_z_only_result_and_a_measure_only_result_have_no_guard (`crates/post/tests/probe_rotated_wcs.rs`) · merges when reviewed\n\nNotes:\n\n- The branch name is not the name in the claim comment. The first push (`issue/2683-probe-rotated-wcs`) had a wrong commit: `upstream/main` moved during the gate, and my `git reset --soft upstream/main` put a revert of other merged work into the commit. No PR used that branch. I deleted it and pushed the correct commit as `issue/2683-probe-rotation-guard`. PR #2711 has 10 files and 250 added lines only.\n- What I found: a target work offset whose rotation differs from the active one gets a wrong origin. That is proved by algebra. For equal rotations (the target can be the active work offset), the result depends on a rule of `G10 L20` that the public reference does not state. I did not confirm it on a control.\n- The fix is a refusal at run time: the program tests the rotation registers and ends with `M2` and a message before the write. A Z-only result is not guarded.\n- A run on PathPilot Hub can show if the guard can be relaxed to \"the two rotations differ\". `docs/decisions/2683-probe-rotated-wcs.md` has the details.\n",
    "author_association": "COLLABORATOR",
